     Test: duration 60, window 540-600 → scheduled at 540-600.\n\n## Room gaps\n\n- [ ] Two events in the same room require >= gap minutes between end of one and start of next.\n      Test: default gap 15; two 30-min events in a 1-room scenario scheduled with >=15 min gap, not back-to-back at 0 gap.\n- [ ] Custom gap value is honored (e.g. gap=0 allows back-to-back; gap=60 forces bigger separation).\n      Test: gap=0 → two events back to back (end === next start) is valid & achieved when needed to fit both.\n- [ ] Gap only applies within the same room — different rooms can have events at identical/overlapping times.\n      Test: 2 rooms, 2 events with attendees fitting both, expect both scheduled at same startTime in different rooms.\n\n## Prerequisites\n\n- [ ] Event B with prerequisite A is scheduled so A.endTime + gap <= B.startTime.\n      Test: A (30min) then B depends on A; assert ordering & gap in output.\n- [ ] Event with unscheduled prerequisite (e.g., prereq itself unschedulable) → dependent also unscheduled.\n      Test: A impossible to schedule (attendees too large) → B (prereq A) also lands in unscheduled.\n- [ ] Multiple prerequisites: event must start after the LATEST of all prerequisite end times (+gap).\n      Test: C depends on A and B, ends of A/B differ → C.startTime >= max(A.end,B.end) + gap.\n- [ ] Prerequisite referencing unknown event id → treated as impossible, event goes to unscheduled (not a crash).\n      Test: event with prerequisites: ['ghost'] → unscheduled, no throw.\n\n## Cycle detection\n\n- [ ] Self-reference (A depends on A) throws an Error synchronously when `schedule()` is called.\n      Test: expect(() => schedule(...)).toThrow().\n- [ ] Two-node cycle (A→B→A) throws.\n      Test: expect(() => schedule(...)).toThrow().\n- [ ] Longer cycle (A→B→C→A) throws.\n      Test: expect(() => schedule(...)).toThrow().\n- [ ] Valid DAG (no cycle) with shared prerequisites does NOT throw.\n      Test: diamond dependency A→B, A→C, B→D, C→D schedules without throwing.\n\n## Preferred room\n\n- [ ] Event with preferredRoom that has capacity & availability → assigned to that room.\n      Test: 2 rooms both viable, event prefers room 2 → assignment.roomId === room2.id.\n- [ ] Event with preferredRoom lacking capacity → falls back to another valid room instead of going unscheduled.\n      Test: preferredRoom too small, another room big enough → scheduled in the other room.\n- [ ] Event with preferredRoom unknown id → falls back to normal room selection, no crash.\n      Test: preferredRoom: 'nonexistent' → still scheduled in a valid real room.\n\n## Batch optimization (global, not greedy-only)\n\n- [ ] A scenario where naive first-come-first-served ordering would strand a schedulable event,\n      but a smarter assignment schedules more events overall — scheduler achieves the higher count.\n      Test: construct case (e.g. one large room + one small room, a big event and two small events where\n      greedy placement of big event first blocks smaller ones from fitting) — assert unscheduled.length\n      is minimized / assert total scheduled count meets the known-optimal number.\n- [ ] `unscheduled` array contains exactly the event ids not present in `assignments`, and every event id\n      appears in exactly one of the two arrays (no duplicates, no omissions).\n      Test: for a mixed scenario, assert assignments.map(a=>a.eventId) ∪ unscheduled === all event ids, disjoint.\n\n## Output shape / misc\n\n- [ ] `assignments[i].endTime === assignments[i].startTime + event.duration` for every assignment.\n- [ ] No two assignments in the same room violate the gap constraint (exhaustive pairwise check) — property test.\n- [ ] Empty events array → `{ assignments: [], unscheduled: [] }`.\n- [ ] Empty rooms array with non-empty events → all events unscheduled, no throw.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
