+# Contract — Constraint-Based Event Scheduler
+
+Definition of done: every checkbox below has a passing test in `src/scheduler.test.ts`,
+`npm run build` compiles cleanly, and `npm run lint` passes with zero errors.
+
+## Core scheduling
+
+- [x] Single event, single room, fits in window → scheduled with correct start/end times.
+      Test: 1 event (30min), 1 room, window 540-600 → assignment startTime=540, endTime=570.
+- [x] Multiple independent events fill distinct rooms/slots without overlap.
+      Test: 2 events needing same room, no overlap in output; or 2 rooms, 2 events, each gets one.
+- [x] Event that cannot fit in the time window (duration > window) → unscheduled.
+      Test: event duration 600, window is 60 minutes wide → unscheduled.
+- [x] Default `attendees` is 1 when omitted.
+      Test: event w/o attendees fits a room with capacity 1.
+
+## Room capacity
+
+- [x] Event attendees > every room's capacity → unscheduled.
+      Test: event attendees=50, all rooms capacity<50 → in `unscheduled`.
+- [x] Event only fits a subset of rooms (by capacity) → scheduler picks a room satisfying capacity.
+      Test: 2 rooms (cap 2, cap 10), event attendees=5 → assigned to the cap-10 room.
+
+## Time window
+
+- [x] Assignment startTime >= options.startTime and endTime <= options.endTime always.
+      Test: generate several events/rooms, assert bounds on every assignment.
+- [x] Event exactly fitting the window boundary (duration === endTime-startTime) is scheduled.
+      Test: duration 60, window 540-600 → scheduled at 540-600.
+
+## Room gaps
+
+- [x] Two events in the same room require >= gap minutes between end of one and start of next.
+      Test: default gap 15; two 30-min events in a 1-room scenario scheduled with >=15 min gap, not back-to-back at 0 gap.
+- [x] Custom gap value is honored (e.g. gap=0 allows back-to-back; gap=60 forces bigger separation).
+      Test: gap=0 → two events back to back (end === next start) is valid & achieved when needed to fit both.
+- [x] Gap only applies within the same room — different rooms can have events at identical/overlapping times.
+      Test: 2 rooms, 2 events with attendees fitting both, expect both scheduled at same startTime in different rooms.
+
+## Prerequisites
+
+- [x] Event B with prerequisite A is scheduled so A.endTime + gap <= B.startTime.
+      Test: A (30min) then B depends on A; assert ordering & gap in output.
+- [x] Event with unscheduled prerequisite (e.g., prereq itself unschedulable) → dependent also unscheduled.
+      Test: A impossible to schedule (attendees too large) → B (prereq A) also lands in unscheduled.
+- [x] Multiple prerequisites: event must start after the LATEST of all prerequisite end times (+gap).
+      Test: C depends on A and B, ends of A/B differ → C.startTime >= max(A.end,B.end) + gap.
+- [x] Prerequisite referencing unknown event id → treated as impossible, event goes to unscheduled (not a crash).
+      Test: event with prerequisites: ['ghost'] → unscheduled, no throw.
+
+## Cycle detection
+
+- [x] Self-reference (A depends on A) throws an Error synchronously when `schedule()` is called.
+      Test: expect(() => schedule(...)).toThrow().
+- [x] Two-node cycle (A→B→A) throws.
+      Test: expect(() => schedule(...)).toThrow().
+- [x] Longer cycle (A→B→C→A) throws.
+      Test: expect(() => schedule(...)).toThrow().
+- [x] Valid DAG (no cycle) with shared prerequisites does NOT throw.
+      Test: diamond dependency A→B, A→C, B→D, C→D schedules without throwing.
+
+## Preferred room
+
+- [x] Event with preferredRoom that has capacity & availability → assigned to that room.
+      Test: 2 rooms both viable, event prefers room 2 → assignment.roomId === room2.id.
+- [x] Event with preferredRoom lacking capacity → falls back to another valid room instead of going unscheduled.
+      Test: preferredRoom too small, another room big enough → scheduled in the other room.
+- [x] Event with preferredRoom unknown id → falls back to normal room selection, no crash.
+      Test: preferredRoom: 'nonexistent' → still scheduled in a valid real room.
+
+## Batch optimization (global, not greedy-only)
+
+- [x] A scenario where naive first-come-first-served ordering would strand a schedulable event,
+      but a smarter assignment schedules more events overall — scheduler achieves the higher count.
+      Test: construct case (e.g. one large room + one small room, a big event and two small events where
+      greedy placement of big event first blocks smaller ones from fitting) — assert unscheduled.length
+      is minimized / assert total scheduled count meets the known-optimal number.
+- [x] `unscheduled` array contains exactly the event ids not present in `assignments`, and every event id
+      appears in exactly one of the two arrays (no duplicates, no omissions).
+      Test: for a mixed scenario, assert assignments.map(a=>a.eventId) ∪ unscheduled === all event ids, disjoint.
+
+## Output shape / misc
+
+- [x] `assignments[i].endTime === assignments[i].startTime + event.duration` for every assignment.
+- [x] No two assignments in the same room violate the gap constraint (exhaustive pairwise check) — property test.
+- [x] Empty events array → `{ assignments: [], unscheduled: [] }`.
+- [x] Empty rooms array with non-empty events → all events unscheduled, no throw.
